Let's find this limit directly.

To do that, we will want to divide both the numerator and the denominator by the same quantity, in a way that will help us derive the limit.

Since the leading term of the numerator is $x^2$, let's divide by $x^2$.

In the denominator, let's divide by $\sqrt{x^4}$, since for any value, $x^2=\sqrt{x^4}$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{6x^2-x}{\sqrt{9x^4+7x^3}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{6x^2-x}{x^2}}{\dfrac{\sqrt{9x^4+7x^3}}{\sqrt{x^4}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide sides by }x^2=\sqrt{x^4}}
\end{align}$

Now let's continue by simplifying the expression and using the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{6\cancel{x^2}}{\cancel{x^2}}-\dfrac{1\cancel x}{\cancel x \cdot x}}{\sqrt{\dfrac{9\cancel{x^4}}{\cancel{x^4}}+\dfrac{7\cancel {x^3}}{\cancel {x^3}\cdot x}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{6-\dfrac{1}{x}}{\sqrt{9+\dfrac{7}{x}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{6-0}{\sqrt{9+0}}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{6}{\sqrt{9}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{6}{3}
\\\\
&=2
\end{align}$
